WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.465
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.465 --> 00:00:04.437
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:06.902
[CLICKING]

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.010
PROFESSOR: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:20.970
So in this lecture, we talk
about the Higgs mechanism.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:22.560
As you might know,
the Higgs boson

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.150
was discovered in 2012
by the LHC Experiment,

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.730
but the theoretical
discovery of the Higgs boson

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.910
happened much, much
earlier than that did.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:36.810
In the mid 1960s, Peter
Higgs and a few others

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:39.510
proposed a mechanism
which gives rise

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:43.770
to masses of the gauge
boson, the w and the z boson.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.540
And the Higgs boson,
or the Higgs field

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:49.020
then can also be used to
give masses to the fermions.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.450
So let's have a look
at this and start

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.850
with a simple observation.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:58.020
When we have written down our
Lagrangian for a simple spin

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:02.430
one field, gauge
field, like a photon,

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:06.300
we find that we want
to have local gauge

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.250
invariant for this
equation, which

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:09.750
means that we can
do a local gauge

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:13.770
transformation of our
fields, and the physics

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.370
should be unchanged of this.

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:18.960
So the physics, meaning that the
description by the Lagrangian,

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:23.680
should be invariant under
this transformation.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:24.280
All right?

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:26.230
The problem, however,
is that, if you

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:29.620
want to have a spin one
gauge field which is massive,

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:32.710
you have to have terms in
your Lagrangian, like this one

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:42.920
here, where you have a
mass term for your fields.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:47.890
So in general, this is not
possible without breaking gauge

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:50.710
invariants, and this
is a guiding principle

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:53.060
of our Lagrangian theory.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.570
So this is a real bummer.

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:57.940
So if you want a
stopping point, so you

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:01.690
have a beautiful theory which
describes all the interactions,

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:05.590
but one important
characteristic, the masses

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:08.100
of the particle is missing.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.430
But you are able to
actually do this,

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:15.840
not by adding specific mass term
but by breaking the symmetry,

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.370
by breaking the
local gauge symmetry.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.910
There's various ways to do
this, and one of the ways

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:24.490
is to use spontaneous
symmetry breaking.

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:26.310
So what is spontaneous
symmetry breaking?

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:28.590
Imagine you have a
symmetry of rotation,

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:33.420
a symmetry like this pen here,
and by applying some force

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.640
on top, this pen would bend.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.920
And by bending
this pen, it bends

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.090
in one specific direction, that
breaks the rotational symmetry.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:45.530
Another way to look at this
is to just let this pen drop.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:49.200
Let it go to its ground state,
lowest possible energy state,

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.630
and it will land
somewhere on the table

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:53.940
and by doing this
breaking the symmetry,

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.770
and it does this spontaneously.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:01.460
Let's look at spontaneous
symmetry breaking

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:03.120
in a toy model first.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:04.760
So what we're going
to do here just

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:08.720
add a complex scalar field and
the corresponding potential

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:09.920
for this field.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:17.590
Potential is shown here,
and this general potential

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:19.460
can have multiple forms.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:22.775
The first form would just
be this parabola here.

00:03:22.775 --> 00:03:26.200
This is a solution where mu
square, this term, mu square,

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:27.640
is greater than 0.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:31.310
In this term, there's
this unique minimum.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:36.120
The minimum is here at
0, and because of that,

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:38.750
the mass of this field
would be equal to 0,

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:43.320
and the mas of our gauge field
would also be equal to 0.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:49.155
But what happens now if we
have through this potential

00:03:49.155 --> 00:03:51.260
a breaking of the symmetry?

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:55.030
So in this case here, the
vacuum itself, the lowest energy

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:56.780
state, breaks the symmetry.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:59.020
You go away from the
0 point, and you're

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:01.970
breaking the symmetry.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:07.750
So this minimum is at
v over square root 2.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:11.710
v is the vacuum expectation
value of this field,

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:13.870
and you can simply
rewrite then this field

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:17.720
itself by evolving it
around its minimum.

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:22.030
And so you find two fields
here, this chi and this h.

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:24.940
The h is already kind of
pointing towards the Higgs

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:28.900
boson, and the vacuum
expectation value.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:31.362
Now, if you add this back
into your Lagrangian--

00:04:31.362 --> 00:04:32.320
and I'll do this again.

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:35.730
This is shown here, but
also on the next slide--

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:37.710
you can start to
identify terms which

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.600
look like mass terms
for your particle.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:44.740
And the first one
is here which can

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:46.990
be identified as a mass
term for our gauge field.

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:49.450
The mass is e time v.

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:55.330
e times v is the strength of
the coupling of this gauge field

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:59.680
e times the value of the
vacuum expectation value.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:00.730
All right?

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:01.780
So this is interesting.

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:08.170
So we used this new scalar
field to break spontaneously

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:10.480
the symmetry, and
then the mass term

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:12.730
appears which is
proportional to the strength

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:16.480
of the coupling and the
vacuum expectation value.

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:21.910
So the mass is generated
through the spontaneous symmetry

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:25.720
breaking and the
coupling to the field.

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:28.060
You also find a mass
term for the six field

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:29.500
here, for this h field.

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:30.730
This is not the Higgs boson.

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:32.530
It's just a field
which looks like it,

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:33.960
and so this mass term is here.

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:37.870
But remember that mu
squared is less than 0,

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:42.600
and then this chi, or the
so-called Goldstone boson,

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.680
its mass is 0.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:46.870
But then we have those
terms left over here,

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:49.800
which we cannot really
interpret it very well.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:56.770
And it's possible to remove them
by choosing a specific field.

00:05:56.770 --> 00:06:01.140
So we do a gauge transformation
by just relabelling things,

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:05.100
and then the new Lagrangian
is independent of this field.

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:10.470
Just as a reminder, Goldstone,
the Goldstone boson,

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:14.070
you find those Goldstone bosons
in many places in physics.

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:17.610
And Jeffrey Goldstone is
a retired faculty at MIT,

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:21.270
so you might, in the spring
or next summer, you'll him

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:24.920
walking across the corridors.

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:29.430
So we find our new Lagrangian
which has our mass terms here,

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:32.030
which has a term
for the Higgs field,

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:35.520
and has our potential
for the Higgs field.

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:38.180
So this specific gauge
we just decided to use,

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:41.270
this so-called unitarity
gauge, and it's

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:44.210
important to note that the
Lagrangian itself contains

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:46.550
all physical particles.

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:49.650
But this chi, the
Goldstone boson, is gone,

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:52.040
and the lingo we
sometimes use here

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:53.690
is that the Goldstone
boson has been

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:56.240
eaten by the physical bosons.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:59.900
And the way it has
been eaten is through

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:02.160
the longitudinal
polarization of this boson.

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:06.260
It's the equivalent of saying
that it has acquired mass.

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:10.710
So the pocket guys here for
spontaneous symmetry breaking

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:15.770
is such that spontaneous
symmetry breaking of a u1 gauge

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:19.100
symmetry by a non-zero
vacuum expectation

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:22.350
value of a complex scalar
results in a massive gauge

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:25.820
boson and one real
massive scalar field.

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:29.090
So we created mass,
but as a side product,

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:31.040
we also have an
additional field.

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:35.830
And that field itself has a
mass term, so it's massive.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:38.710
The second scalar we
had just disappeared.

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:40.810
The Goldstone boson
has been eaten

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:47.590
by the longitudinal component
of the gauge field itself.

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:49.550
All right.

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:51.530
That was a simplified toy model.

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:54.240
Let's look at the
standard model.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:57.060
So now, here, we have
to generalize from u1

00:07:57.060 --> 00:08:00.030
to su2 or su-n gauge groups.

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:03.210
The scalar field is now an
n-dimensional fundamental

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:06.660
representation of that group
for the standard model.

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:09.360
That will be su2.

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:12.990
The gauge fields are n squared
minus one-dimensional joint

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:16.930
representations,
like our photon,

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:23.080
for example, or our
unmixed w boson field.

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:25.870
And the Lagrangian looks
very similar to the one we

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:28.540
just before with our potential.

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:30.040
Again, we have this
mu squared term.

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:36.570
We also have a lambda term here,
and then we require local gauge

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:39.520
invariants again.

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:40.690
OK.

00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:43.210
So now, for the standard
model, again, su2

00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:45.610
cross u1 gauge groups.

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:50.500
We introduce a complex field,
complex six field in su2.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:54.330
It's a duplex, meaning that
it has four components.

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:56.950
It's complex and
has two components,

00:08:56.950 --> 00:09:00.280
which there's a total of
four components to it.

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:02.800
So we already know that we
want to use m square less

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:07.750
than 0 for our potential to
allow for spontaneous symmetry

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:09.580
breaking to occur.

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:17.480
The minimum is then at
1 over square root 2.

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:20.930
0 for the upper component,
and v, the vacuum expectation,

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:22.220
for the lower component.

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:23.525
That's a choice already.

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:29.810
Again, why mu
square less than 0?

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:35.960
Because if we would have chosen
to use a positive value for mu

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:38.590
square, we wouldn't have
spontaneously broken

00:09:38.590 --> 00:09:39.930
the symmetry.

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:40.430
OK?

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:42.560
So we need to have
potential which looks

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:45.890
like this Mexican hat here.

00:09:45.890 --> 00:09:46.400
All right.

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:50.120
So now, what happens now
to our w and z bosons.

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:54.640
We have discussed
electroweak mixing already.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:55.140
Good.

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:56.265
So that was the first step.

00:09:56.265 --> 00:09:59.510
Now, we will understand
where the mass terms actually

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:01.550
come from.

00:10:01.550 --> 00:10:07.270
So now, we just did the
spontaneous symmetry breaking,

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:09.910
and now we are looking at what
happens now if we also couple

00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:11.920
the Higgs field to the bosons.

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:16.020
So again, we write
this this way,

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:19.580
and then we just
try to find terms.

00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:21.260
It's really like a
mechanical writing

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:23.840
of the individual terms,
and you find again

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:26.870
terms which have a vacuum
expectation value here

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:28.820
and the coupling here.

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:33.230
And you find the coupling to
the u1 term and the coupling

00:10:33.230 --> 00:10:37.730
to the su2 and the coupling
to the u1 term representative

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:41.240
to the coupling to the
original photon field

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:45.390
and our gauge field for su2.

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:46.200
All right.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:49.620
The rest is rewriting
and identifying terms.

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:52.080
If you do this-- and this
is like a couple of pages

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:53.620
of writing, fine--

00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:55.860
but if you do this, you
find, again like before,

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:58.560
that you find the first
and second component

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:00.570
of our su2 gauge field.

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:05.580
It gives us the charge at the
boson, the w plus and the w

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:06.420
minus.

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:08.640
And then the z
boson and the photon

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:15.030
mixtures of the third
component and the field b.

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:16.080
All right?

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:18.930
So these are all
physical fields,

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:22.860
and then we try to identify the
mass terms you find for the w.

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:25.830
That the w mass is
proportional or equal

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:33.630
to the coupling strands of
the su2 group times the vacuum

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:35.610
expectation value over 2.

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:38.610
And the mass of the
z boson is given

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:45.600
by both couplings times
the square root of the sums

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:49.350
of the square times v over 2.

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:50.160
OK.

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:52.560
If you're trying to look for
a mass term for the photon,

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:56.310
you find none, meaning that
the photon is massless.

00:11:56.310 --> 00:11:59.110
And then we can look again
at weak mixing angles,

00:11:59.110 --> 00:12:01.160
and they are now
defined directly

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:04.560
through the couplings in
those two gauge groups.

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:06.900
The masses of the
w and the z bosons

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:12.312
are related via this weak
mixing angle, cosine theta w.

00:12:12.312 --> 00:12:13.400
All right.

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:16.020
Those elements we
already saw before.

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:18.850
Now, we find that the
masses of the gauge bosons

00:12:18.850 --> 00:12:21.180
are given by
spontaneous symmetry

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:24.260
breaking via the vacuum
expectation value

00:12:24.260 --> 00:12:27.710
and the strength of the
coupling of the gauge field

00:12:27.710 --> 00:12:31.510
to the Higgs field.

00:12:31.510 --> 00:12:32.080
All right.

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:36.550
So in summary, we started
with a complex scalar field.

00:12:36.550 --> 00:12:41.130
A representation of su2 is
four degrees of freedom.

00:12:41.130 --> 00:12:44.550
The Higgs vacuum expectation
value breaks the symmetry

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:46.260
spontaneously.

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:50.760
The w plus and w minus and
the z boson require mass,

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:54.480
and the three Goldstone
bosons are each absorbed

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:57.540
into the w's and the z bosons.

00:12:57.540 --> 00:13:00.270
We also find an
additional scalar Higgs

00:13:00.270 --> 00:13:02.770
boson that remains.

00:13:02.770 --> 00:13:07.110
And so that was
the understanding

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:09.300
in the '60s and '70s,
and the standard model

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:10.770
was further developed.

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:12.930
And then it took us
all the way to 2012

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:15.650
to actually find this
new scalar particle,

00:13:15.650 --> 00:13:18.110
the Higgs boson itself.

